= a
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The <Mordell-Weil theorem> gives $E(\mathbb Q)\cong E(\mathbb Q)_{\rm tors}\oplus\mathbb Z^r$. Move the rational point of order two to $(0,0)$ and clear denominators to obtain an integral <Weierstrass equation of an elliptic curve>
$$
E:y^2=x(x^2+ax+b),\qquad b(a^2-4b)\ne0.
$$
The <two-isogeny formula> gives $E':Y^2=X(X^2-2aX+a^2-4b)$ and <dual isogenies> $\phi:E\to E'$, $\widehat\phi:E'\to E$ with $\widehat\phi\phi=[2]$. Compute the <torsion subgroup> using <reduction of torsion points on an elliptic curve> at several good primes, followed by <division polynomials of an elliptic curve> to identify the possible points.

For the free part, use <two-isogeny descent>. The <two-torsion square-class homomorphism> is
$$
\alpha(O)=1,\qquad \alpha((0,0))=[b],\qquad \alpha((x,y))=[x]
$$
in $\mathbb Q^*/\mathbb Q^{*2}$, with <kernel> $\widehat\phi E'(\mathbb Q)$; define $\alpha'$ on $E'$ similarly. The <prime-support bound in two-isogeny descent> restricts the first image to signed square-free divisors of $b$ and the second to those of $a^2-4b$. For each candidate $d$, solve its <two-isogeny descent quartic>
$$
N^2=dU^4+aU^2V^2+\frac bd V^4.
$$
Test <local solubility> at the real place and the relevant <p-adic fields>, discard impossible classes, and search the surviving coverings for <rational points>. A point with $UV\ne0$ gives $x=d(U/V)^2$, $y=dUN/V^3$. Include the identity and rational <2-torsion> cases separately. Once both rational images are determined, the <two-isogeny rank formula> gives
$$
2^r=\frac{\#\alpha(E(\mathbb Q))\,\#\alpha'(E'(\mathbb Q))}{4}.
$$
The point searches give explicit candidates for a basis. Their <canonical height pairing> can certify independence; <Mordell-Weil saturation> and effective height bounds then certify that they generate the full free part. Rank alone does not certify generators of the whole <Mordell-Weil group>. If the rational images are not fully determined, the procedure instead supplies lower and upper bounds on the <rank of an elliptic curve>.

= b
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The obstruction is the <local-to-global gap in isogeny descent>. A <two-isogeny descent quartic> can have points over $\mathbb R$ and every <p-adic field> without a <rational point>. Local tests compute an <isogeny Selmer group>, which can strictly contain the rational-point quotient. For an <isogeny of elliptic curves> $\phi:E\to E'$, the comparison is
$$
0\longrightarrow E'(\mathbb Q)/\phi E(\mathbb Q)
\longrightarrow\operatorname{Sel}^{\phi}(E/\mathbb Q)
\longrightarrow\operatorname{Sha}(E/\mathbb Q)[\phi]
\longrightarrow0.
$$
The last group is the kernel of $\phi:\operatorname{Sha}(E/\mathbb Q)\to\operatorname{Sha}(E'/\mathbb Q)$, so it records elements of the <Tate–Shafarevich group> invisible to all local tests. Searching a locally soluble covering which has no <rational points> never succeeds. Thus a <Selmer group of an elliptic curve> gives an upper bound and found independent points give a lower bound, and these need not coincide. Higher descent can improve the bounds, but this particular procedure has no unconditional guarantee of closing every gap.

= c
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

For $E:y^2=x^3-17x$, the <two-isogeny formula> gives $E':Y^2=X^3+68X$. By the <prime-support bound in two-isogeny descent>, the image of the <two-torsion square-class homomorphism> on $E$ lies in $\{1,-1,17,-17\}$. The identity gives $1$, $T=(0,0)$ gives $-17$, and $P=(-1,4)$ gives $-1$, since $(-1)^3-17(-1)=16$. The image is a <subgroup> of the <square-class group>, so their product $17$ occurs as well; explicitly $P+T=(17,68)$. Hence
$$
\alpha(E(\mathbb Q))=\{1,-1,17,-17\}.
$$
On $E'$, a nonzero affine point has $X>0$, because $Y^2=X(X^2+68)$. The only candidate <square classes> are therefore $1,2,17,34$. The identity gives $1$, $T'=(0,0)$ gives $[68]=17$, and $(2,12)$ gives $2$, since $2^3+68\cdot2=144$. Their product $34$ occurs; adding $T'$ gives $(34,-204)$. Thus
$$
\alpha'(E'(\mathbb Q))=\{1,2,17,34\}.
$$
Both images have order four. The <two-isogeny rank formula> proves
$$
\boxed{2^r=\frac{4\cdot4}{4}=4,\qquad \operatorname{rank}E(\mathbb Q)=2.}
$$
This is the <rank-two elliptic curve with cubic x cubed minus seventeen x>; all candidate classes are represented, so no unresolved <local-to-global gap in isogeny descent> remains in this computation.

= 2
{parent=Paper 21}
